explanation:
This route order gives validation a chance to stop the request.
```javascript
app.use(express.json());
app.post("/labels", requireLabel, (req, res) => {
  res.status(201).json({label: req.cleanLabel});
});
```

This small call shows the successful continuation path:
```javascript
function requireLabel(req, res, next) {
  if (typeof req.body?.label !== "string") return res.status(400).json({error:"Label required"});
  const clean = req.body.label.trim();
  if (!clean) return res.status(400).json({error:"Label required"});
  req.cleanLabel = clean;
  next();
}
const request = {body:{label:"  Sky  "}};
requireLabel(request, {}, () => console.log("Continue"));
console.log(request.cleanLabel);
```
This prints Continue, then Sky. trim takes no arguments and returns a string without outer whitespace. The callback passed as next takes no input here and prints Continue. In Express, the framework supplies next to continue to later middleware. Rejected input must send its response and return without calling next. The empty response object is sufficient only for this successful local demonstration, not for testing the rejection path.

