explanation:
JSON object keys and string values use double quotes.
```javascript
const text = '{"city":"Oslo"}';
const place = JSON.parse(text);
console.log(place.city);
```
This prints Oslo.

Parsing can succeed without giving you the shape you need.
```javascript
console.log(JSON.parse("null"));
console.log(JSON.parse("[2, 5]"));
```
The first value is null; the second is an array, not a city record.

A guard can reject those cases before reading a property.
```javascript
function hasCity(value) {
  if (value === null || typeof value !== "object" || Array.isArray(value)) return false;
  return typeof value.city === "string";
}
```

After defining hasCity, inspect both paths directly:
```javascript
console.log(hasCity({city:"Oslo"}));
console.log(hasCity(null));
console.log(hasCity([]));
```
The results are true, false, false. The helper receives an unknown value and returns whether it is a non-array record with a string city; it does not parse JSON or change the input.

Malformed JSON throws an error. A catch block can handle it instead of stopping the program.
```javascript
try {
  JSON.parse("not JSON");
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Could not read the text");
}
```

